WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.565
I want you to listen and try to guess who

00:00:02.565 --> 00:00:04.858
or&nbsp;what this is.

00:00:04.858 --> 00:00:11.252
(Chirping sound)

00:00:11.252 --> 00:00:12.951
That was bat ultrasound,

00:00:12.951 --> 00:00:16.544
recorded above your hearing range,&nbsp;
but slowed down so you could hear.

00:00:16.544 --> 00:00:20.038
So that was an advertisement call 
from the peak of the&nbsp;mating season.

00:00:20.038 --> 00:00:22.800
Scientists can decode these calls,

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:25.997
so a sample bat to English
 translation would be,

00:00:25.997 --> 00:00:26.879
"Pay attention.

00:00:26.879 --> 00:00:29.887
I'm a Pipistrellus nathusii bat, 
specifically male.

00:00:29.887 --> 00:00:31.101
My name is X.

00:00:31.101 --> 00:00:32.222
I am landing&nbsp;here

00:00:32.222 --> 00:00:34.175
and we share a common social identity

00:00:34.175 --> 00:00:35.705
and common communication pool."

00:00:35.705 --> 00:00:38.558
For a pickup&nbsp;line by a bat, not bad.

00:00:38.558 --> 00:00:42.309
Scientists have recorded millions 
of bat vocalizations like&nbsp;this

00:00:42.309 --> 00:00:44.770
and they've decoded many of them using AI.

00:00:44.770 --> 00:00:47.857
And they've revealed that 
bats have&nbsp;dialects

00:00:47.857 --> 00:00:50.196
that they pass down from 
one generation to the next,

00:00:50.196 --> 00:00:53.479
and that baby&nbsp;bats learn to 
speak just like you did,

00:00:53.479 --> 00:00:55.821
by listening to the adults around them

00:00:55.821 --> 00:00:59.392
and&nbsp;babbling back until they speak adult bat.

